WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:09.530
In 1955, United Airlines flight 629 exploded
in midair above Colorado, sending debris,

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.889
engine parts, luggage, and cargo scattering
across the ground.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:18.600
All 39 passengers and 5 crew members were
killed.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.369
FBI investigators collected the evidence and
performed background checks on the passengers

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:28.349
- slowly putting together a narrative of what
had occurred.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.850
They identified a man named John Gilbert Graham,
who was the life insurance and will beneficiary

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.010
of one of the women on board the plane...
his mother.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.390
He held a grudge against her for placing him
in an orphanage as a child.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:51.030
Christ-on-a-cracker, well Graham cracker,
you could have just slipped arsenic in her

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.030
tea.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:56.610
When the FBI searched his property they discovered
bomb-making parts identical to those found

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:57.710
in the wreckage.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:03.760
Shockingly, no law at the time existed for
blowing up an aeroplane full of people, so

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.500
Graham was charged with the single premeditated
murder of his mother, despite causing the

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.290
deaths of 43 other people.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.420
He was found guilty and executed soon after.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.190
For the last 100 years, the FBI has hunted
some of the most dangerous criminals on the

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.190
planet.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:29.740
Thieves, serial killers, gangsters, and terrorists
have all found themselves in the crosshairs

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.210
of America’s finest FBI agents.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:40.079
These days, the FBI employs roughly 35,000
people across 55 field offices in the US,

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:42.430
and one in Puerto Rico.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:46.969
Aside from the Special Agents -which Hollywood
has shown us in everything from Silence of

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:54.450
the Lambs to Die Hard- the FBI employs scientists,
intelligence analysts, support professionals,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.820
information technology experts, and many more…
all of whom come together for one reason and

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:06.509
one reason only: to hunt down criminals and
bring them to justice.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:14.300
But impressive as this 35,000-strong army
of seasoned manhunters is today, there was

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.910
a time not so long ago when nothing like the
FBI even existed.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:25.540
In 1908, Attorney General Charles Bonaparte
met President Theodore Roosevelt at a Civil

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:32.140
Service reform meeting in Baltimore in what
was a very different USA to the one we know

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.210
today.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:37.150
Innovations like the railroad had helped the
rapid spread of people throughout the country

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:42.870
during the nineteenth century, supporting
a population explosion.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.470
And it wasn’t only the nation’s cities
that were mushrooming - the crime rate was,

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:48.470
too.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.871
But at the time, there was almost no way to
enforce the law on such a grand scale, with

00:02:53.871 --> 00:02:58.240
most police forces focusing at the community,
or sometimes state-level.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:05.450
At that meeting in Baltimore, President Roosevelt
instructed Charles Bonaparte - who bore a

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.820
striking resemblance to Poirot and was, randomly
enough, the great nephew of French general

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.240
and emperor Napoleon Bonaparte - to change
all that.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:20.820
As progressives, the two men were united by
their politics and the belief that ability,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:26.120
rather than political connections, should
be the deciding factor in the recruitment

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.410
of law enforcement personnel.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:34.500
Such was their dedication to the ideal of
a true meritocracy that after Roosevelt boasted

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:41.000
he had dished out border patrol jobs to candidates
with the best marksmanship rather than the

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:48.210
most influential mates, Bonaparte responded
- jokingly, I hope - by suggesting Roosevelt

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:54.920
should instead have had his candidates shoot
at each other and given the jobs to the survivors.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:03.210
Under Roosevelt’s authority, on 26th July
1908 Bonaparte formed a corps of special agents

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.910
that would soon be known as the Bureau of
Investigation.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:14.360
Led by Chief Examiner Stanley Finch, the Bureau’s
first ever director, this team of just 34

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:20.940
people were the pioneers of what would go
on to become the FBI we know today.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:26.440
This might not sound like much, but back in
1908, creating an operation like this in America

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.280
was controversial.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:33.850
Much like today, the US was pretty big on
its freedoms and many people didn’t like

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.560
the idea of an oppressive government ruling
across state lines.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:45.480
So introducing an agency of law enforcement
experts with a remit to do exactly that wasn’t

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.340
met with cheers and applause - and many citizens
weren’t cooperative with investigations.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.840
In those early days, the Bureau of Investigation
primarily spent its time dealing with civil

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:03.530
rights cases including antitrust, land fraud,
banking fraud, copyright violations, and forced

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.210
labor, as well as the odd national security
threat.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:13.040
. But when it was asked to take the investigative
lead on the newly-passed Mann Act, also known

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.000
as the White Slave Traffic Act, its power
and influence began to grow…

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.660
Which was lucky, because the coming years
were going to present the young agency with

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.630
one hell of a challenge.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.840
In many ways, the Bureau was formed at just
the right time.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:36.210
In 1908, a nine year old kid by the name of
Alphonso Capone was already making a name

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:41.490
for himself on the streets of Brooklyn; John
Dillenger, just five at the time, was living

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.930
his best life on his parents’ farm in Indiana,
and Dillinger’s future partner in crime

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:53.960
- literally - Lester Joseph Gillis, though
you probably know him by his nickname, Baby

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.660
Face Nelson - would be born within the year.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:04.910
Thanks in no small part to that motley crew,
1921 to 1933 were years so rife with gangsters

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:10.940
and flagrant disregard for Prohibition, which
had kicked in in 1920, that they’re often

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.160
called The “Lawless Years.”

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:19.270
And, although the Bureau had swollen to some
300 agents and an additional 300 support agents

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:24.180
off the back of some early successes, they
would have to get creative if they were going

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.710
to prevail in the war on crime.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:30.240
And get creative they did.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:37.500
Al Capone, all grown up and now sporting the
catchy nickname ‘Scarface’ had reigned

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:43.380
supreme at the head of the “Chicago outfit”
for 7 years when he had 7 members of rival

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:50.070
North Side gang executed by firing squad on
Valentine’s Day in 1929.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.460
The old romantic.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:57.390
But Capone was a smart man, and though it
was the worst kept secret in Chicago that

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:01.820
he was behind the hit, there was little evidence
on which to indict him.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:06.310
Capone did end up in the slammer later that
year when he was caught carrying a concealed

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:11.270
deadly weapon - but he was back out on the
streets again within 9 months thanks to - you

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:14.500
really can’t make this stuff up - good behaviour.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.740
Unsurprisingly, the Bureau were less than
satisfied with this outcome, and determined

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.730
to nail their man they opened an investigation
against him.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:28.480
In the end, they got him on tax evasion of
all things.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:36.130
Because yes, believe it or not illegally earned
income is subject to income tax in the US.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:40.800
It just goes to show, you can have people
brutally murdered by firing squad…

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.940
But don’t you dare try and cheat the IRS.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:48.050
Another big scalp came when the FBI took on
the Klu Klux Klan.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:52.820
After falling into relative obscurity towards
the end of the nineteenth century, the bedsheet-wearing

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:57.669
racists were enjoying something of a resurgence
by the nineteen twenties.

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:04.140
Under the guiding hand of Edward Young Clarke,
the Klan were once again rising to prominence,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.710
boasting one million new members.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:13.030
For his efforts, Clarke was granted the title
“Imperial Kleagle,” a senior position

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.960
in the Klan but not quite a Grand Wizard.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.460
Apparently looking like a bunch of kids dressing
up for halloween wasn’t enough for these

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:23.590
guys - they had to give themselves dungeons
and dragons nicknames as well.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:29.360
Despite the cross-burnings, murders, and lynchings,
the Bureau wasn't able to investigate Clarke,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.789
since none of the laws he was breaking were
federal.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:36.910
That is, until some enterprising young agent
had an idea.

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:42.180
You see, Clarke had been doing very well out
of the huge number of new initiates joining

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:49.020
the Klan, personally profiting from 80% of
every 10 dollar membership fee.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:53.800
With his pockets full of cash - wait, do bed
sheets have pockets?

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.110
With his... pillowcases?...

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:02.050
full of cash, he did what so many rich men
in positions of power had done before - he

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.770
got himself a mistress.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:09.690
But when he took said strumpet on a jaunt
over state lines, the Bureau realised they

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.930
had him in violation of the Mann act.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:17.470
Because, hey, feel free to recruit people
to your racist organisation, but don’t you

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.820
dare try and get laid on a dirty weekend away
with your side chick.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:28.020
On May 10th, 1924, the Bureau appointed a
new head - a man by the name of John Edgar

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.890
Hoover.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:35.990
Like his predecessors, Hoover dreamed of making
the Bureau a shining example of a meritocracy.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:41.060
He immediately fired agents he believed were
underqualified, and only promoted the most

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:43.500
effective to senior positions.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.100
He implemented regular office inspections
to keep his men on their toes, , and rigorous

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.260
training courses were introduced for new agents.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:58.060
An early success of this new regime came when
Hoover established the Bureau’s identification

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:02.000
division, with the aim of better keeping track
of criminals.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.950
At the time,the majority of fingerprint records
were stored locally from city to city, and,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:13.920
astonishingly, the first use of a centralised
system was housed in a prison and staffed

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.310
by convicts.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:21.440
Which was about as good an idea as building
a jelly bean factory in an obesity clinic.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:27.480
In 1926, Hoover had the collection moved to
Washington under Bureau administration, and

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.750
soon after, other agencies began supplying
their data.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:39.100
A few years later in 1929, the American stock
market went, as we say in the UK, tits up.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:44.090
Like many catastrophes, the Great Depression
proved to be fertile ground in which criminals

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:45.960
thrived..

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:51.920
One of them, John Dillinger, had swapped milking
cows on his parent’s farm in Indiana for

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.160
something a little more exciting - bank robbery.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:11:00.770
And he wasn’t emptying vaults, Dillinger
and his gang took time off to murder people

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:06.850
and stage jailbreaks for their incarcerated
pals.Dillinger was seen as a Robin Hood-type

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:13.160
hero by some, and when he was shot and killed
by the Bureau’s agents, his legend was,

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.200
if anything, bolstered.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.300
But so was the Bureau’s.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:22.310
Thanks to stopping Dillinger and others like
him, the organisation became a household name

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:27.220
for the first time, and it’s agents began
appearing in popular culture.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:33.240
To capitalise on this newfound prestige, in
1935, Hoover felt like it was time for a rebrand

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:39.820
- he decided the Federal Bureau of Investigation
had a certain ring to it.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:46.260
He was right - the FBI is now probably the
most famous intelligence agency worldwide.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:52.530
But there was no time for backslapping and
soggy biscuit, because across the Atlantic,

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:57.170
an angry Austrian named Adolf Hitler had recently
risen to power.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:01.600
As World War II broke out across Europe, the
United States remained neutral.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.380
But a similar brand of fascism to that which
had taken root in Hitler’s Germany found

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:12.730
itself at home in a depression-stricken America.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:19.300
There was increased racial and labour unrest
and the American Communist Party saw its influence

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.300
grow.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:26.589
Frightened by the potential for subversion
on a mass scale, in 1939 a Presidential direction

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:32.790
was signed that permitted the FBI to investigate
political agitators.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:38.399
As the war escalated, the FBI’s role expanded
to track down any “potentially dangerous”

00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:46.030
citizens with antecedents from Axis nations
- that is, people of German, Italian, or Japanese

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:47.030
heritage.

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:53.649
After Pearl Harbour was attacked, FBI agents
rounded up over 110,000 Japanese Americans

00:12:53.649 --> 00:13:00.140
and held them as prisoners of war - all under
the order of President Franklin D. Roosevelt.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:06.290
In June 1942, two German submarines covertly
deposited agents on US soil.

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:10.690
Evading the coast guard, they made their way
inland and began to blend in with the locals.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:16.560
But one of the agents, a man named George
Dasch, grew so terrified of capture that he

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:23.851
turned himself in to the FBI, passing on information
that led to the capture of the rest of the

00:13:23.851 --> 00:13:24.851
agents while he was at it.

00:13:24.851 --> 00:13:30.270
The prisoners were tried shortly afterwards,
and those that were uncooperative were executed.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:36.060
The FBI’s swift work in this case and others
like it helped to bolster American confidence

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:43.890
in their ability even further, and bBy the
end of 1943, the FBI’s numbers had swelled

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.080
to over 13,000 employees.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:53.120
The 1960s were a particularly turbulent time
for the FBI, and the United States in general.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.410
President Kennedy was assassinated, crime
was on the rise, and violence against the

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.870
establishment was becoming ever more common.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:05.180
The Vietnam war was met with widespread protest,
and not just in the form of peace signs.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:15.820
In 18 months between 1971 and 1972, the FBI
recorded 2,500 bombings, almost five per day.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:20.550
The Civil Rights Movement spearheaded by figures
like Martin Luther King and Malcolm X was

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:25.990
also well underway, and Hoover initiated a
secret counterintelligence program to spy

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:30.310
on and disrupt so-called radical organisations.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:37.590
This program, named COINTELPRO, heavily targeted
King and Malcolm X, while in the open, Hoover

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:42.300
criticised the new emerging forms of protest
used by the civil rights movement and others

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:48.740
as “a conspiracy reflected by questionable
moods and attitudes, by unrestrained individualism,

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:54.830
by nonconformism in dress and speech, even
by obscene language, rather than by formal

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:57.430
membership in specific organizations.”

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:03.070
So, in other words, if you weren’t boring,
you were a commie.

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:08.680
Of course, it wasn’t until 2015 when we
finally discovered the link between vibrant

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:10.860
hair dye and communism – if only Hoover
would have known he could have saved himself

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:11.860
a buck or two.

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:16.170
This period also saw the FBI encounter a whole
host of bizarre new cases that would test

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:18.269
their agents to their limits.

00:15:18.269 --> 00:15:25.290
In 1962, three men mysteriously vanished from
Alcatraz prison - a case that remains unsolved

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:26.870
to this day.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:32.959
In 1968, the serial killer known as Zodiac
arrived on the scene and taunted the authorities

00:15:32.959 --> 00:15:37.540
and media with cryptic messages and grisly
murders.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:43.750
On December 8th, 1963, three men kidnapped
Frank Sinatra junior, son of the legendary

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:49.280
singer and actor, holding him ransom in the
hope of making some cash out of ol’ blue

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:50.310
eyes’ himself..

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:55.310
The kidnappers were stopped by police while
Sinatra junior was stuffed in the trunk, but

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:59.560
they managed to bluff their way back to their
hideout.

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:04.540
Frank senior coughed up and paid the ransom,
but the kidnappers were caught by FBI agents

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:09.470
soon afterwards and most of the cash was recovered.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:15.370
The 70s and 80s saw the FBI establish new
national priorities: foreign counterintelligence,

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:19.780
organised crime,white-collar crime and counterterrorism.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:25.000
The last of which was about to become the
priority number one, when a homemade explosive

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.440
was detonated at Chicago university…

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:35.760
The first attack in what would become a 17-year
reign of terror perpetrated by the man known

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:36.760
as the Unabomber.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.250
But more on him later…

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:45.410
One of the strangest cases of the 70s was
that of student Patty Hearst, who was kidnapped

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:51.000
in 1974 by the Symbionese Liberation army
- which sounds like a political group from

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.710
Monty Python’s the Life of Brian, but was
actually a terrorist group seeking all-out

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.660
war against the US capitalist state.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:03.470
But things got a little weird when footage
emerged of Hearst swearing allegiance to the

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:10.279
very Symbionese Liberation Army she had been
abducted by, and they got weirder still when

00:17:10.279 --> 00:17:15.489
she was seen robbing a bank later that year
weilding an M1 carbine.

00:17:15.489 --> 00:17:22.110
By all accounts, it seemed like she’d been
working with the People’s Symbionese Front

00:17:22.110 --> 00:17:26.720
– I mean Symbionese Liberation Army – all
along.

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:32.129
The FBI tracked the SLA to a warehouse where
a massive shootout occurred and, when the

00:17:32.129 --> 00:17:36.490
safe house caught fire, 6 SLA members were
burned alive….

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:39.970
But Hearst wasn’t among them.

00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:44.279
She had escaped, but the FBI was hot on her
tail.

00:17:44.279 --> 00:17:50.549
On September 18th, 1975 she was caught and
charged with robbery.

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:55.000
During her trial, the defence claimed she
had been brainwashed, referring to her as

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:01.070
a ‘low-IQ zombie’, but the jury found
her guilty all the same, sentencing her to

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.279
35 years in prison, later reduced to 7.

00:18:04.279 --> 00:18:09.419
She was later pardoned by President Clinton
– but then the philanderer did have a penchant

00:18:09.419 --> 00:18:15.799
for pardoning terrorists, especially comunist
terrorists, he granted so many grot bags and

00:18:15.799 --> 00:18:21.429
bombers clemency it became a scandal called
Pardongate.

00:18:21.429 --> 00:18:25.629
During the 1980s, against a backdrop of repeated
bombings by the Unabomber, the illegal drug

00:18:25.629 --> 00:18:31.799
trade was on the rise and the FBI was awarded
joint jurisdiction with the DEA over narcotics-related

00:18:31.799 --> 00:18:32.799
crimes.

00:18:32.799 --> 00:18:37.970
Concurrent to this, the FBI was growing more
sophisticated in its forensic methods, with

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:44.539
breakthroughs in DNA technology helping to
reliably identify or rule out suspects.

00:18:44.539 --> 00:18:51.470
This in turn created a new DNA index -similar
to the fingerprint index- which helped keep

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.669
track of dangerous suspects.

00:18:54.669 --> 00:18:59.690
Despite the technological breakthroughs, the
FBI was still unable to catch the Unabomber,

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:04.940
a failing that was widely condemned in the
media, as was a failed raid of a religious

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:12.029
sect in Waco Texas that led to 80 people,
including children, being burned alive.

00:19:12.029 --> 00:19:16.970
By the time the 90s came around, international
crime was becoming a serious business and

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:23.279
the FBI director at the time, Louis Freeh,
even travelled to Sicily in 1993 and publicly

00:19:23.279 --> 00:19:27.609
compelled the Sicilian people to break free
of the mafia's grip.

00:19:27.609 --> 00:19:31.149
All while the mob watched from the crowd.

00:19:31.149 --> 00:19:38.380
The 90s was also when the FBI finally managed
to gain some traction with the Unabomber case...

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:44.730
Between 1978 and 1995 the Unabomber had carried
out a series of bombing attacks, killing 3

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.860
people and injuring 23 others.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:54.489
A 150-strong task force had been created by
the FBI to find him, without success.

00:19:54.489 --> 00:20:00.159
He left no forensic evidence, created bombs
from readily available scrap metal, and chose

00:20:00.159 --> 00:20:02.570
his victims at random.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:06.289
But his luck was about to change...

00:20:06.289 --> 00:20:12.970
In 1995 the Unabomber released a 30,000-word
manifesto expressing a desire to strike out

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:19.570
against modern technology and advocating a
return to primitive society and nature.

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:24.289
After much deliberation, the FBI released
the manifesto to the press in the hopes that

00:20:24.289 --> 00:20:27.789
someone would recognise the killers' words.

00:20:27.789 --> 00:20:29.770
And someone did.

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:36.619
David Kaczynski contacted the FBI and described
how he had a troubled, loner brother named

00:20:36.619 --> 00:20:38.080
Ted.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:44.139
He described how his brother was something
of a genius, taught at the University of California

00:20:44.139 --> 00:20:50.580
(which had been the target of two bombs),
and had since retired to a log cabin in Montana.

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:56.700
Providing letters written by his brother Ted,
David gave FBI linguistic analysts all the

00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:03.389
evidence they needed to identify Ted Kaczynski
as the Unabomber.

00:21:03.389 --> 00:21:09.340
On April 3, 1996, The Unabomber Ted Kaczynski
was arrested in his cabin.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:13.429
At the scene, a live bomb was found ready
for mailing.

00:21:13.429 --> 00:21:19.730
After 17 years one of the FBI’s most persistent
threats had been captured - a result of cutting

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:24.859
edge linguistic analysis and hard-won public
cooperation.

00:21:24.859 --> 00:21:31.350
In recent times, the FBI has faced terrorist
threats off the back of 9/11 and ISIS, increased

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:35.200
cyber-crime, and the mystery of Hilary Clinton's
emails.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:43.509
It has also successfully captured 463 of the
494 most wanted fugitives of the noughties

00:21:43.509 --> 00:21:50.639
As of August 2020, 94% of the most wanted
fugitives ever identified have been captured

00:21:50.639 --> 00:21:51.899
or located.

00:21:51.899 --> 00:21:56.049
Not bad for a group that started out as a
handful of ex-cops.

